content.thinking:
Interesting! So mri:
1. When parsing `--base-url`, it only sets `base-url` in the output (not camelCased)
2. When parsing `-b`, it sets both `b` and `baseUrl` (the alias is expanded)

Now let me trace through the CAC code:

1. After mri parsing, there's a camelCase transform:
```javascript
parsed = Object.keys(parsed).reduce(
  (res, name) => {
    return {
      ...res,
      [camelcaseOptionName(name)]: parsed[name],
    }
  },
  { _: [] }
)
```

So `{ 'base-url': 'https://gitlab.com' }` becomes `{ 'baseUrl': 'https://gitlab.com' }`

2. Then defaults are set for ALL names:
```javascript
for (const cliOption of cliOptions) {
  if (!ignoreDefault && cliOption.config.default !== undefined) {
    for (const name of cliOption.names) {
      options[name] = cliOption.config.default
    }
  }
}
```

So `options['b'] = 'https://github.com'` and `options['baseUrl'] = 'https://github.com'`

3. Then the parsed values are set:
```javascript
for (const key of Object.keys(parsed)) {
  if (key !== '_') {
    const keys = key.split('.')
    setDotProp(options, keys, parsed[key])
  }
}
```

So `setDotProp(options, ['baseUrl'], 'https://gitlab.com')` is called, which sets `options.baseUrl = 'https://gitlab.com'`.

But `options.b` is never updated because mri only returned `baseUrl` in the parsed output (after camelCase transform).

So the fix should be: when setting default values, we should only set the default for names that are NOT already present in the parsed options. Or more precisely, we should only set defaults for names that are not aliases of an already-parsed name.


The real issue is that when one alias is explicitly parsed (like `--base-url`), we shouldn't be setting defaults for any of the other aliases of that same option. The default should only apply when the entire option is missing from the input, not when one form of it is provided but others aren't.

